The current branch, from magit.

** Why magit and not `vc-mode'
`vc-mode' is a variable: it records *that* a buffer is under version
control, not which branch. Getting a branch name out of the stock machinery
means `vc-git-diff-index' or a `git rev-parse' — a subprocess. On a phone,
spawning git is the single most expensive thing a status bar could do, and
doing it per redisplay is not an option.

magit already keeps the branch in memory, so reading it is a hash lookup
rather than a subprocess — magit pays for the git call once per repository
and this segment only ever reads its cache. It is refreshed on save, on
commit, and by the bar's shared cache tick, so the branch is right the
instant you commit without paying for a git call on any redisplay. If magit
cannot answer, the bar falls back to the stock `vc-mode' value rather than
going blank.

** Dirtiness is not this segment's job
The design called for a "dirty accent" on the branch. It lives in the
`modified' segment instead, and deliberately so: `mode-line-modified' is a
stock variable, so Emacs re-evaluates it on every redisplay and the accent
tracks the buffer with no trigger and no subprocess. Folding it into the
branch would mean either a baked, stale accent or a git call per redisplay.
The bar shows dirtiness on the left, next to the buffer it belongs to.

** Segment
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(defvar manifolding-modeline--vc-cache nil
  "Cached branch name for the current buffer, or nil when there is no repository.")

(defun manifolding-modeline--vc-refresh ()
  "Recompute `manifolding-modeline--vc-cache'. Never signals."
  (setq manifolding-modeline--vc-cache
        (or (manifolding-modeline--vc-branch)
            ;; Fall back to whatever Emacs itself records, so a machine
            ;; without magit still shows that the buffer is versioned.
            (and (stringp vc-mode)
                 (not (string-empty-p vc-mode))
                 vc-mode))))

(defun manifolding-modeline--vc-branch ()
  "Current branch according to magit's caches, or nil if magit cannot say.
Guarded on both availability and failure: magit is a normal package that
can be missing, out of date, or pointed at a repository it has not opened."
  (when (fboundp 'magit-get-current-branch)
    (condition-case nil
        (let ((branch (magit-get-current-branch)))
          (when (and (stringp branch) (not (string-empty-p branch)))
            (string-trim branch)))
      (error nil))))

;; Immediacy. The shared cache tick would catch these within 30s, but a
;; branch that is stale right after a commit is the one moment you read it.
(add-hook 'after-save-hook #'manifolding-modeline--vc-refresh)
(add-hook 'vc-post-checkin-hook #'manifolding-modeline--vc-refresh)

(manifolding-modeline-define-segment vc
  :doc "Current branch from magit, cached on save and commit."
  :face manifolding-modeline-info
  :side :right
  :construct 'manifolding-modeline--vc-cache
  :refresh '(manifolding-modeline--vc-refresh))
#+end_src
